22:03:05 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s understandable to worry about a child starting school, but it’s best not to assume he will struggle because he’s a boy. Children’s readiness and learning styles vary widely within every gender, and describing boys as “clueless” can unintentionally lower expectations. She can support him by focusing on his individual needs: - **Build confidence and curiosity:** Talk positively about school, read together, let him ask questions, and praise effort, persistence, and problem-solving rather than “being smart.” - **Create predictable routines:** Consistent sleep, morning, homework/reading, and bedtime routines make school demands easier to manage. - **Practice practical independence:** Let him practise dressing, opening lunch containers, packing his bag, following simple instructions, taking turns, and asking an adult for help. - **Read and talk every day:** Shared reading, storytelling, word games, and conversations about his day build language skills that support learning across subjects. - **Support movement and play:** Active play, sports, drawing, building, and hands-on activities can develop attention, coordination, self-regulation, and confidence. - **Stay connected with the teacher:** Early, friendly communication helps identify what is going well and any specific areas where he needs support. Ask for concrete observations rather than relying on broad assumptions about boys. - **Watch for persistent difficulties:** If he consistently struggles with attention, speech/language, reading, behavior, anxiety, hearing, or vision, she can speak with the teacher and his pediatrician or health professional. Early support is useful and doesn’t mean anything is “wrong.” The most helpful message for him is: *“School is a place to learn; you don’t have to know everything already, and we’ll help you when things are hard.”*
